# R4: read-only final return verification

R4 is only for the `resident-verify` stage after the reviewed R2 RAM worker has
returned to the selected249 resident firmware. The other four stages keep the
already reviewed, unchanged R3 operator. R1/R2/R3, every math/Qfplib byte, firmware
and dataset remain unchanged. R4 contains no transition, flash, UI setter or key
action; it uses the frozen R2 read-only capture and unchanged R3 live lease,
native transport, pinning and owned process supervisor.

Root accepted that the physical B6 input changed volatile `VIEW=0` to `VIEW=1`
before the fresh current26 capture, while the firmware initializes the view to0
on boot. A fresh Root grant may explicitly contain
`transient_UI_view_disposition=VIEW1_RETURN_TO_DEFAULT0_ACCEPTED_NO_EXACT_UI_CLAIM`.
Only in this post-return verifier, that exact disposition admits a single
`VIEW=1` to `VIEW=0` token change. The rest of the UI row, all other settings,
registers, stack, program, calculator IP, working directory and alarms must
match exactly. Any other difference or a missing disposition produces HOLD.
Exact original settings remain accepted without an exception.

An admitted view reset is recorded as `UI_VIEW_RESET=true`, `UI_exact=false`
and an explicit UI acceptance HOLD; it never becomes a claim of exact UI
restoration. No effort is made to send a key or save the view. The already
adopted RTC_SET0 placeholder remains a separate fresh-base clock HOLD with no
setter and no claim of calendar continuity. Master13 restoration is deferred.

The three inert controls cover missing Root disposition, a changed second UI
field and a changed calculator IP. They are pure JSON comparisons and do not
open hardware, run ARM, rebuild firmware or repeat mathematical qualification.
The envelope is a source/evidence binding, not a physical stage grant. Actual
execution still requires Root's fresh exact canonical lease and argv.
